<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Habesh Eyalet</h1><p class="st-meta">1555 CE – 1865 CE (approx.) · capital Massawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ottoman province on the Red Sea coasts of Eritrea, Sudan and Arabia, created to fight the Portuguese and control the trade of the Horn.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Portuguese fleets entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha">Ozdemir Pasha</a>, who in 1555 created the province of Habesh (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Abyssinia</a>) and in 1557 seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dahlak">Dahlak</a> islands from the Sultanate of Dahlak. He hoped to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a>, and the province's seat moved between Massawa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> held the coast, collecting customs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade, and allied at times with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/medri-bahri">Medri Bahri</a> against the Ethiopian emperors. Inland campaigns failed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarsa-dengel">Sarsa Dengel's</a> victories in the 1570s and 1580s. Day-to-day authority on the African coast passed to the Naibs of Arkiko, local Beja governors who ruled in the sultan's name.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The province was much reduced over time, and in 1865 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Italians later took Massawa in 1885, a step towards <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/habesh-eyalet.jpg" alt="Habesh Eyalet" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orwellianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(1844%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1555 CE – 1865 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Massawa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ozdemir Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ottoman Turkish, Arabic, Tigre</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eyalet of Habesh, Ottoman Abyssinia, Habeshistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1710&amp;focus=habesh-eyalet" aria-label="Habesh Eyalet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5756 1598 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Habesh Eyalet lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6076 1854L6061 1872M6076 1854L5709 970M6076 1854L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6061 1872h0M5709 970h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6076 1854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6076 1854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Massawa. <a href="/atlas?year=1710&amp;focus=habesh-eyalet">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
